#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064988} :activity/id #uuid "b3e69442-b9dc-56f2-b6f4-b2d9169e67ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048434 :topic/id #uuid "9ef976b2-cf49-59ec-9689-0ec6f4a92d3c" :topic/math-academy-id 288} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064988 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065346 :tutorial/content "The **mean value theorem** states that for any function that is continuous and differentiable on some interval, there is some point where the derivative of the function is equal to the slope between the endpoints. More precisely, that is:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a function that's continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, then there exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}.\n> $$\n\nLet's illustrate this idea with an example. Consider the graph of the function \n\n$$\ny = f(x) = 6 - \\dfrac 4 x\n$$\n\nthat's defined on the closed interval $[1,4].$\n\n![](images/fd/fd4d75f2ed0db427c2e3bd41335ceb302c3126781f595c8c21ec914e50f82f64.png)\n\nThe line $l_1$ is the secant line that intersects the curve at the endpoints of the interval. The mean value theorem guarantees that there is some special point inside the interval where the tangent line has the same slope as $l_1.$ \n\nIn the diagram above, the special point is $(2,4),$ and the tangent line $l_2$ at this point is parallel to the secant line $l_1.$\n\nTo double-check that $l_1$ and $l_2$ are parallel, we can compute their slopes. The line $l_1$ passes through the points $(1,f(1))$ and $(4,f(4)),$ so its slope is given by \n\n$$\nm=\\dfrac{f(4)-f(1)}{4-1} =  \\dfrac{5-2}{4-1} = 1.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the slope of the line $l_2$ can be found by calculating $f'(2).$\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{4}{x^2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(2) = 1\n$$\n\nSince the lines $l_1$ and $l_2$ have the same slope, they are parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "baf78b35-04c6-5d1c-913f-e0a3da70cd8f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2659 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "10d30ee9-1327-5e6c-8d54-1b994e2a9962" :step/math-academy-id 12770 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064990}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064989 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064159 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064041 :question/id #uuid "4bbaf6b0-1057-5485-a3d8-2126e74fecdc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3717"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370618}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "50436e6a-bace-51f7-97a8-73a9025692ab" :step/math-academy-id 12771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064991}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064160 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064042 :question/id #uuid "ed796d54-235b-5ec0-b73f-5396d1ef99b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3718"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050572}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341952} {:db/id 